# USAGE: After editing this file, to recreate and reload the
# ~/.config/run-or-raise/shortcuts.conf file, run the OMR
# 'install' task:
#
#   mr -d "${DOPP_KIT:-${HOME}/.kit}/odd/gnome-extensions/run-or-raise" -n install
#
# - SAVVY: The 'install' task combines the two shortcuts sources:
#
#     ~/.depoxy/ambers/home/.config/run-or-raise/shortcuts-depoxy
#     ~/.depoxy/running/home/.config/run-or-raise/shortcuts-client
#
#   to create the run-or-raise config file:
#
#     ~/.config/run-or-raise/shortcuts.conf
#
#   and then toggles the extension so that it reloads the config:
#
#     gnome-extensions disable "run-or-raise@edvard.cz" &&
#      gnome-extensions enable "run-or-raise@edvard.cz"

# ================================================================= #

# REFER: The following comments are adapted from an example
# config (note that FIVERs are the DepoXy author's thoughts):
#
#   https://github.com/CZ-NIC/run-or-raise
#
#   https://github.com/CZ-NIC/run-or-raise/blob/main/shortcuts.default

# The shortcuts may be defined in two ways:
#
# 1. Run-or-raise form: shortcut,command,[wm_class],[title]
#    * `wm_class` and `title` are optional and case sensitive
#    * if none is set, lowercased launch `command` is compared
#      with lowercased windows wm_classes and titles
#
# 2. Run-only form: shortcut,command
#
# The full form is: shortcut[ char][:mode],command,[wm_class],[title]
#    * `char` allows you to use layered shortcuts
#    * `mode` is used to further specify circumstances
#
# - REFER: Find wm_class by using Looking Glass:
#   Press <Alt-F2>, run 'lg', then click Windows to see list of
#   open windows and their wm_class IDs.
#   - X users can use `xprop` instead.
#
# - REFER: modes:
#   https://github.com/CZ-NIC/run-or-raise?tab=readme-ov-file#modes
#
#   - One such mode, switch-back-when-focused, will switch
#     back to the previous window if there's only one app.
#     window, e.g., consider this binding:
#       <Shift><Super>f:switch-back-when-focused,firefox,firefox-esr
#     If there's only one Firefox window and you run this
#     binding, it'll either switch to the Firefox window,
#     or it'll switch back to the MRU app's window. But if
#     there is more than one Firefox window, it'll cycle
#     through all the Firefox windows.
#     - UCASE: The best use case for this mode is if you
#       know that an application will only ever have one
#       window, and then the binding works like a nice
#       focus/unfocus control. [Though the author generally
#       prefers a focus/minimize toggle instead (i.e.,
#       ~/.depoxy/ambers/bin/windows/toggle-visibility does).]
#       But if an application might have multiple windows,
#       I'd suggest not using this mode, because the
#       behavior is different depending on how many app
#       windows you've opened (and I personally don't
#       like the disparity).
#
#   - TSHŌT: Use the :verbose mode for a debug-info toast,
#     e.g.,
#       <Shift><Super>f:verbose,firefox,firefox-esr
#     - Though I'm not sure how useful these details will
#       be (so far, the DepoXy author hasn't needed to use
#       this mode).

# =================
# Run or raise form
# =================
#
# This line cycles any firefox window (matched by "firefox" in the
# window title) OR if not found, launches new firefox instance.
#
# - ILIKE: This is like a nifty app-window Alt-Tabber, albeit it fronts
#   on every press, unlike GNOME's "Switch windows of an app directly"
#   (defaults <Alt-F6>, via /org/gnome/desktop/wm/keybindings/cycle-group)
#   which doesn't change window order while you're previewing windows.
#
#  <Super>f,firefox,,
#
# This cycles any open gnome-terminal OR if not found, launches new one.
# - Ubuntu 17.10: wm_class = Gnome-terminal
# - Debian 13:    wm_class = org.gnome.Terminal
# - Arch:         wm_class = gnome-terminal-server
# - REMEM: Use Looking Glass to glean the Window Manager Class,
#   <Alt+F2> → [Enter] lg → [Click] Windows
#
#  <Super>r,gnome-terminal,org.gnome.Terminal

# You may use regular expression in title or wm_class.
# Just put the regular expression between slashes.
# E.g. to jump to pidgin conversation window you may use this line
# (that means any windows of wm_class Pidgin, not containing the title Buddy List)"
#
#  <Super>KP_1,pidgin,Pidgin,/^((?!Buddy List).)*$/

# Have the mail always at numpad-click.
#
#  <Super>KP_2,chromium-browser --app=https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/#inbox,mail.google.com,

# =============
# Run only form
# =============
#
# REFER: Run-only is of the form: keybinding-comma-command

# This line will launch the `notify-send` command.
#
#  <Shift><Super>y,notify-send "Salut, tout le monde"

# This layered shortcut works only when you type `hello`
# after hitting <Super>e.
#
#  <Super>a h e l l o,notify-send Layered hello
#
# A similar layered shortcut that works when you press
# <Super-a> followed (quickly) by <Super-r>.
#
#  <Super>a <Super>r,notify-send Layered 'Terminal was not launched.'
#
# Note you can use the same prefix for multiple bindings, and
# they won't interfere with one another (though the RoR timeout
# between inputs is pretty quick, so if you type <Super-a> but
# pause briefly before <Super-r>, then this command runs, and
# not the binding above; but if you type <Super-a> <Super-r>
# quick enough, the previous binding runs, not this one).
#
#   <Super>a,gnome-terminal,org.gnome.Terminal

# =================
# REFER: Add't help
# =================

# TSHŌT: If RoR cannot bind to a specific keybinding, it'll
# alert you via a popup notification (toast), e.g.:
#
#   Unable to grab accelerator: <Control><Super>2
#
# The (DepoXy) author has only seen this happen with "hidden"
# keybindings that GNOME claims for opening the Nth application
# from the Dock (Dash), specifically the <Cmd-1>..<Cmd-9> and
# <Shift-Cmd-1>..<Shift-Cmd-9> bindings.
# - You can disable these bindings by running the following:
#     for ord in {1..9}; do
#       dconf write "/org/gnome/shell/keybindings/switch-to-application-${ord}" "@as []"
#       dconf write "/org/gnome/shell/keybindings/open-new-window-application-${ord}" "@as []"
#    done
# - See also an Anisble task the author uses, at the bottom of this file:
#   https://github.com/landonb/zoidy_matecocido/blob/1.4.0/tasks/keybinding-circus.yml#L236-L243

# ================================================================= #
# DEPOXY ACCELERATORS

# Cycle Firefox windows (using firefox-esr wm_class), or start Firefox.
#
# BNDNG: <Shift-Cmd-F>
<Shift><Super>f,firefox,firefox-esr

# Use <Shift-Cmd-W> to front Google Chrome.
# - If Google Chrome is not active, and if one or more
#   Chrome windows are visible, it'll front the MRU
#   visible window.
#   - Otherise, if no Chrome windows are visible,
#     it'll unminimize one of them.
# - Once Google Chrome has focus, running this binding
#   will bring another Chrome window to the front,
#   which might unminimize a minimized window (and it
#   won't cycle through visible windows first).
# - If you don't want to start Chrome if not running,
#   you could omit the command portion, e.g.:
#     <Shift><Super>t,,google-chrome
# - If you run the author's Hammerspoon bindings on
#   macOS, you'll find it has a similar binding.
#   - The macOS binding only brings Chrome to the
#     front; it does not cycle through Chrome windows.
#   - So this GNOME Shell binding only works the
#     same if Google Chrome is not already active;
#     once Chrome is active, the GNOME binding
#     cycles windows, unlike the macOS binding.
# - I tried the isolate-workspace mode (which restricts
#   windows to those on the current workspace), but it
#   includes minimized windows. E.g.,
#     <Shift><Super>f:isolate-workspace,firefox,firefox-esr
#
# BNDNG: <Shift-Cmd-W>
<Shift><Super>w,/usr/bin/google-chrome --profile-directory=Default,google-chrome

# FEATR: Open Firefox window to manage GNOME Shell Extensions.
# - Opens *GNOME Shell Extensions > Installed Extensions*
# - CALSO: Compare to GNOME Settings accelerator
#   at <Shift-Cmd-Alt-comma>.
# REFER: REMEM: FRMAT: SHORTCUT[ char][:mode],COMMAND,[wm_class],[TITLE]
# BNDNG: <Shift-Ctrl-Cmd-Comma> (<Shift-Ctrl-Cmd-,> <C-D-lt>)
<Shift><Primary><Super>comma,/usr/bin/firefox --new-window https://extensions.gnome.org/local/,,Installed Extensions

# FTREQ: Raise-or-lower instead of raise.
#   ~/.kit/sh/raise-or-lower/bin/raise-or-lower
# BNDNG: <Shift-Ctrl-Cmd-X>
# - Altly: Always raise:
#   <Shift><Primary><Super>x,spotify,Spotify
<Shift><Primary><Super>x,/home/user/.kit/sh/raise-or-lower/bin/raise-or-lower spotify

# BNDNG: <Shift-Ctrl-Alt-O>
# - Mnemonic: Dunno, "o" as in circled?
<Shift><Primary><Alt>o,/home/user/.depoxy/ambers/bin/gnome/toggle-focus-mode

# BNDNG: <Shift-Cmd-Ctrl-Q>
# - Dunno, <Ctrl-Cmd-Q> is locks, but <Shift>ing doesn't? Seems weird.
# CXREF: ~/.depoxy/ambers/bin/gnome/toggle-idle-delay
<Shift><Primary><Super>q,/home/user/.depoxy/ambers/bin/gnome/toggle-idle-delay

# BNDNG: <Shift-Alt-Y>
<Shift><Alt>y,/bin/bash -c "/home/user/.depoxy/ambers/bin/windows/toggle-visibility '- YouTube - Google Chrome' || /bin/sensible-browser --new-window 'https://www.youtube.org/' --profile-directory=Default"

